### Go detections recorded in the repository

`judge/go-mutation-report.tsv` is the committed record of a `judge/mutate_go.py` campaign (macOS arm64, go1.27.2, `-race`,
30 concurrent runs per cell, controlled 1, the 12 mutations of `MUTATIONS`, gate S01 S02 S10 S11 S19 S20 S21). It is separate
from `judge/mutation-report.tsv` (Pekko) and its counters are never added to the Pekko ones. Each row carries the campaign commit
(`go_commit`), the tree of `pekko-actor/` at that commit, the digest of the mutation definition, of the comparator and of the
preserved logs, the Go version and the platform. These values prove identity and coherence; the workspace being clean is
recorded only by `mutate_go.py` refusing to write a report from a `+dirty` workspace.

`scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` reports five facts that are never merged: designed (11 mutations + 1 benign), detected,
valid without detection, invalid (`invalid-campaign`, `harness-error`, `timeout`; visible, never a detection), and
incomplete or without evidence. Calibration (`calibration*.tsv`) and parity (the Go gate) are reported on their own lines.
S20 is a parity cell: its rows are accepted, but a designed detection cannot be attributed to it because none exists, and
it has no Go calibration.

Campaign of `e9c6bac` (all 12 met): 11/11 designed mutations detected by their designed clauses, GMbenign survives in every
cell, 0 invalid, 0 incomplete. This is one platform (macOS); Linux is not measured. It does not establish full pekko-actor parity.

**First Linux campaign (`ba287d8`, #1463).** The first Go campaign on Linux (linux/amd64, go1.27.2, `-race`, 30 concurrent runs, 15 scenarios, 475 rows: 71 failed, 402 survived, 2 excluded, 0 timeouts, 0 harness errors) met all 19 of its mutations (the 18 designed ones and the benign one) and the checker reports `goPekko CURRENT on linux/amd64 ... detected 18/18; benign survived 1/1`. It is retained by the tag `evidence/go-campaign-ba287d8` (full SHA `ba287d81d86d5f3dd99a0671089dae76f85e8b1d`); its provenance, the hashes of the package and the earlier attempts are in `migration/stage-1/judge-anchors-1463/PROVENANCE.md`. It supersedes `b1887c4` as the CURRENT record; the campaigns described above and the row "Recorded Go mutation campaign" of section 10 are earlier campaigns, kept as history. One platform, one campaign; it is not full `pekko-actor` parity, and no area state in this document changed.

**Historical versus current.** The checker prints two lines per platform and never merges them. HISTORICAL is the aggregate of
the Go reports available in the repository (today one, macOS arm64; the line names the platforms and says when there is no Linux
report: it is not Linux evidence). It counts detections that were valid with the comparator and the Go core of the campaign. CURRENT counts only rows that were measured with every input of the campaign as HEAD has it: `tree` is the git tree of the whole `pekko-actor/` directory at HEAD (`git rev-parse HEAD:pekko-actor`, as `judge/mutate_go.py` `provenance` and `scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` compute it): the Go core, its tests, the judge harness `judge_internal_test.go`, `go.mod`, `go.sum` **and `pekko-actor/README.md`**, so any change to a tracked file of that directory, a README edit included, changes it; `comparator_sha256` is the digest at HEAD of `judge/evaluate.py`, `judge/go_scope.py`, `judge/go-scope.json`, every file of `judge/scenarios/`, the campaign script `judge/mutate_go.py` (what is run, how a cell is judged, what counts as a detection) and `.go-version`; and `go_version` is the toolchain `.go-version` pins. The `definition_sha256` of each mutation is checked separately and a stale one rejects the row. Conservative: any change to a spec or to the script counts, relevant or not. Not an input: the platform (a row names it; a second platform is a second report), the documentation outside `pekko-actor/` and the selftests (the documentation inside `pekko-actor/` is part of `tree`, see above). When the runtime, the rules or the specs change, the rows stay as history and CURRENT drops to none until the campaign is
run again: they are never presented as evidence about the new code. `judge/selftest_go_report.py` checks this distinction.

**Preserving the logs.** The hashes of the TSV prove identity and coherence, not the logs. `mutate_go.py --revalidate <workdir>
<report>` is read-only: it re-judges preserved logs and prints the digest of the comparator it used next to the digests recorded
in the rows. With the same digest it is an exact historical reproduction. With another one it says it is a re-evaluation with a
different comparator, not a historical reproduction, and a verdict that differs is attributed to the comparator, never assumed to
be altered logs (only a logs-digest mismatch says that). To reproduce the campaign exactly, run it from a checkout of the tag
`evidence/go-campaign-e9c6bac` (`PROVENANCE.md`). The logs are not committed. The evidence package
(`go-campaign-e9c6bac.tar.gz`, 5,584 files, 642,209 bytes, sha256 `bef637b826cbfca6ca7bce819a1ef178aba7a1e4c0b937dfcdf51664884a5bd8`,
plus `.sha256`, a per-file `SHA256SUMS` and `PROVENANCE.md`) is built deterministically and is prepared but NOT published yet.
The proposed place is a GitHub release attached to the tag `evidence/go-campaign-e9c6bac` (which points at the full SHA
`e9c6bac9bcba4d20c1b474c8c1b895fbf1fbb1b1`), with those three files as assets. A squash drops `go_commit` from the history, so
the report stays valid only while an `evidence/*` tag retains that commit.

Not covered by a mutation: an external `Stop` that is ignored makes the scenario wait for `Done` until the deadline, which
the campaign rules classify as a `timeout`, never as a detection. That defect is therefore not claimed as detected.

## 5. Differences and open items

- **D1, retired in part (slice 3c): no hierarchy.** It was the waiver of S10 G5.c in `judge/go-scope.json`. The hierarchy exists and the waiver is gone. What is judge-verified: G5.c, a child's `post-stop` recorded before the parent's `post-stop-begin`, with two children (S10, both modes). What is covered by Go unit tests only: child names, `Done` of a parent implying `Done` of its subtree (D3), exactly-once finalization, stale and duplicate signals. What stays **not implemented**: `Kill` and user-defined strategies (#450) (system `Shutdown` exists since U10, #479, and `AllForOne` since U6, #447) (the guardians exist since U5a to U5c, #463, #465, #467; at slice 3c they did not); supervision with `Resume`, `Restart`, `Stop` and `Escalate` exists for parents that declare a strategy (U1, U2), and the default strategy for the rest (U3; top-level actors are children of `/user` since U5b), and no scenario of the judge covers it. Parity of the hierarchy is not claimed beyond G5.c.
  Historical note (ADR 0014 slice 2): at that point parent/child identity, per-parent names and `Spawn(Spawner, ...)` existed but a stopping parent did not stop its children, so D1 and the waiver were still true. Slices 3a and 3b added the recursive stop and slice 3c removed the waiver, as stated above.
- **D2, retired: stop before the first activation.** Defined here (the first increment, section 5, and ADR 0012; not decision D2 of ADR 0010). It was a known divergence (S20 failing exactly with G5.e): a stop accepted before the first activation ran neither `PreStart` nor `PostStop`, whereas in Pekko `Create` is queued ahead of `Terminate` and both hooks run. [ADR 0012](adr/0012-stop-before-first-activation.md) is **accepted** (option 1, amended by #690 and #695): `finalize` runs the start step first when it has not run (`beginStartLocked`), then `PostStop`, except for an instance whose `PreStart` failed (`initFailed`: Pekko leaves `_actor` null and calls `postStop` only `if (a ne null)`, `dungeon/FaultHandling.scala:247-254`). Evidence: `TestPreStartHooks`, `TestAFailedPreStartKeepsTerminationButSkipsPostStop` and judge scenario S20 (section 9). Limit: the mechanism differs from Pekko (a system message queued ahead of `Terminate` versus a transition inside the first activation), so equivalence is observable, not structural.
- D3, known, narrowed by U1 and U2: a `Receive` error stops the actor unless its parent declares a strategy (then `Resume`, `Restart`, `Stop` or `Escalate`); a parent without a strategy now applies the default strategy (U3), like Classic, for every actor that has a parent; a top-level actor is a child of `/user` (U5b) and is restarted by default, like any child.
- `scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` used to print `goPekko: 0 observed (no runtime yet)` and did not list S19 to S21 in the
  milestone scenario table. Both are resolved in section 8: S19, S20 and S21 are registered, and the Go line is honest about
  what it does and does not derive. It still reads only the Pekko mutation report, so it derives no Go detection.

